## Adversarial finding (FAIL basis): healthcare-entry names are NOT
preserved in general — only in the placeholder-person subcase the fix's own
tests exercise.

inferPositionalHealthcareLinks (internal/services/retirement/settings.go)
links an unlinked healthcare entry to an unlinked person by position+exact
age match, then a documented follow-up only copies the healthcare entry's
name onto the PERSON when the person's name is a placeholder
("you"/"spouse"/"user"/"primary"). But prepare.ComputeAges — called
unconditionally on every load/save, unchanged pre-existing code — then does
`s.HealthcarePersons[i].Name = person.Name` for every LINKED entry,
regardless of whether the name-adoption step ran. So whenever the matched
person's name is NOT a placeholder (i.e. already a real, distinct name), the
healthcare entry's own real name is silently overwritten by the person's
name and the original is lost. This is a genuine regression the positional
linker introduces, not a pre-existing defect: on master, an entry with no
person_id simply stays unlinked and keeps its own name untouched.

Probe (added as a throwaway test in the private copy, mirroring the
worker's own settings_healthcare_link_test.go fixture style):
persons "Robert" (primary, non-placeholder) / "Susan" (spouse,
non-placeholder), healthcare entries "Bob" (age matches Robert) / "Sue" (age
matches Susan), no person_id on either healthcare entry — exactly the shape
inferPositionalHealthcareLinks is designed to catch (1:1 count, exact age
match at the original start date).

$ go test ./internal/services/retirement/ -run TestProbe_NonPlaceholderPersonName_HealthcareNameSurvives -v
    bob: PersonID="p1" Name="Robert" CurrentAge=70   (want Name="Bob", was linked)
    sue: PersonID="p2" Name="Susan" CurrentAge=68    (want Name="Sue", was linked)
    REGRESSION: healthcare entry hc1 linked and its real name was clobbered: got "Robert", want "Bob" preserved
    REGRESSION: healthcare entry hc2 linked and its real name was clobbered: got "Susan", want "Sue" preserved
--- FAIL: TestProbe_NonPlaceholderPersonName_HealthcareNameSurvives (0.01s)

A/B against master (baseline worktree .worktrees/cm1-baseline, 895d509,
copied to a private dir), SAME fixture shape, no positional-link code path
exists:
$ go test ./internal/services/retirement/ -run TestProbe_Baseline_UnlinkedHealthcareStaysUnlinkedAndNamed -v
    hc hc1: PersonID="" Name="Bob" Age=69
    hc hc2: PersonID="" Name="Sue" Age=67
--- PASS

Confirmed this field is genuinely user-visible and user-editable, not an
internal bookkeeping field: web/templates/components/whatif/
healthcare-person.html renders `{{.Name}}` as the card label (line 10) and
exposes it as an editable text input `name="name" value="{{.Name}}"` (lines
57, 63). So the defect is a real, silent, on-save-persisted loss of a
user-entered field — directly contradicting the claim under test: "Linked
entries mirror BirthMonth; user-visible healthcare names survive." That
guarantee only holds in the placeholder-person subcase the worker's own
m5/m6 tests cover (settings_healthcare_link_test.go covers "You"/"Spouse" +
"Darrell Gallion"/"Christine" — the live plan's actual shape — but never a
case where the person already has a real, distinct name). This is squarely
in-scope: inferPositionalHealthcareLinks is new code entirely authored for
this task (CM1.3.files manifest), and the claim under test states the
"names survive" property without the placeholder qualifier.

## Other checker-second attack surfaces investigated (no separate FAIL)

- Split-classification / dual-formatter risk: every save path
  (UpdateSettingsWithPersons, guardrails saves, SaveWithRevision,
  SaveWithRevisionIfScenario) funnels through the single
  `saveInternal` → `resolveCurrentMonth` choke point (grepped: only
  settings.go writes whatif.json; no other WriteFile/MarshalIndent site
  touches WhatIfSettings). `use_current_month:false` is respected at every
  call site because there is exactly one site. No split-classification
  defect found here.
- Migration write-on-first-load failure: `loadInternalContext` propagates a
  `saveInternal` error straight back as the Load error (`if changed { if err
  := sm.saveInternal(settings); err != nil { return nil, err } }`), so a
  read-only settings file WOULD fail the entire page load rather than
  serving the already-correctly-computed in-memory settings. Verified this
  exact code shape is byte-identical on master 895d509
  (.worktrees/cm1-baseline) — NOT a new defect. However CM1 substantially
  widens the blast radius: previously `changed` fired only for rare legacy
  shapes (no Persons, missing healthcare-person links); now it also fires
  for EVERY plan saved before this feature shipped (`use_current_month`
  absent from rawFields), i.e. most/all currently-live plans, on their next
  load. Reporting as a backlog observation per scope rules, not a FAIL: the
  hard-fail-on-migration-write-error mechanism itself is pre-existing and
  out of this task's writeable scope, even though this task increases how
  often it is hit.
- use_current_month:false / stale-date surfaces: all templates that render
  ages (budget-analysis, rmd, healthcare-person, quick-adjust,
  rate-assumptions, scenario-chain-card, social-security, spending-phases)
  read `.Settings.CurrentAge`/`.SpouseAge`/`.CurrentAge` fields populated by
  prepare.ComputeAges post-resolve; none compute age independently. MCP
  plan tools (internal/services/mcpsvc/plan) have no direct StartDate
  handling — they route through the same SettingsManager Load/Save. No
  stale-surface found.
- Age-guard exact-equality: DeriveAgeAtStartDate (internal/models/whatif.go)
  computes integer month difference and floors to years — exact,
  no tolerance window; a person born in the current month ages up exactly
  at that boundary, consistently for both settings.CurrentAge and the
  healthcare-link guard's comparison (both compare against the same
  "YYYY-MM"-granularity dates), so no month-boundary ambiguity distinct
  from ordinary birthday handling was found. Worker's own m6 test
  (TestPositionalHealthcareLinkInference_OffByOneStaysUnlinked) demonstrates
  exact-match is enforced, not tolerant.
- False-link risk from reordering: worker's own m3 test
  (TestPositionalHealthcareLinkInference_CountMismatchStaysUnlinked) covers
  count mismatch. A same-age "twins" case (two different real people who
  happen to be the same age) cannot be distinguished from a correct
  positional pairing by an age-only guard in either order — this is an
  inherent limitation of position+age heuristics, not unique to this
  implementation, and is a narrower risk than the name-clobbering defect
  above (that one fires on ANY exact age match, twins or not).

## Backlog observations (not FAIL grounds — outside this task's writeable scope)
- Migration-write-on-load hard failure (loadInternalContext) is pre-existing
  general behavior (confirmed identical on master 895d509) but this task
  dramatically increases how often a legacy plan hits it (virtually every
  plan saved before use_current_month shipped). Worth a follow-up hardening
  task: on migration-write failure, log and still serve the in-memory
  migrated settings rather than failing the whole load.
